## Batch Operations (Transactional Batches)

> **Note**: Batch operations are atomic and limited to a single partition key, but can include reads, creates, replaces, and deletes.

```typescript
import { BatchOperationType, OperationInput } from "@azure/cosmos";

const batchOperations: OperationInput[] = [
  {
    operationType: BatchOperationType.Create,
    resourceBody: { id: "b1", category: "electronics", name: "Batch Item 1", price: 10 },
  },
  {
    operationType: BatchOperationType.Create,
    resourceBody: { id: "b2", category: "electronics", name: "Batch Item 2", price: 20 },
  },
  {
    operationType: BatchOperationType.Read,
    id: "b1",
    partitionKey: "electronics",
  },
];

const response = await container.items.executeBatchOperations(batchOperations);

response.forEach((result, index) => {
  if (result.statusCode >= 200 && result.statusCode < 300) {
    console.log(`Batch operation ${index} succeeded`);
  } else {
    console.error(`Batch operation ${index} failed: ${result.statusCode}`);
  }
});
```

> ⚠️ Batch operations are **not the same** as bulk operations. Bulk operations handle multiple items across partitions with high throughput, but are not atomic. Use batch only when atomicity is required within a single partition.

